WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.944 --> 00:00:07.150
The road trip from Seattle

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.513
to Vancouver takes about three hours.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:13.670
There's pretty scenery
to look at along the way

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.373
and a really exciting border to cross.

00:00:26.988 --> 00:00:29.180
Once you make it into British Columbia,

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.613
everything changes in an instant.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.163
It's like entering another world.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.712
In Vancouver, most public servants

00:00:38.712 --> 00:00:41.810
are required to drive Teslas,

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.063
so you see the things
just about everywhere.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.523
All of the clocks here run
on steam and play songs.

00:00:59.968 --> 00:01:02.490
And the airports are in the water.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:11.090
Oddities like these aside,

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.023
it would be hard to dream
up a more beautiful city,

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.313
beautiful mountains, beautiful buildings,

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.880
beautiful geese, beautiful
graffiti complimented

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.063
by beautiful, filthy alleyways.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.040
And if you know the right spots,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.113
you can eat like a king in Vancouver.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.473
This counts as a light lunch in Chinatown.

00:01:42.473 --> 00:01:43.556
That is good.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.543
And if you know the right guy
at the right hole in the wall,

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.810
you can get what you need.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:52.643
It's pure.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.640
But I'm in Vancouver for more

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.753
than just mainlining maple syrup.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.260
There are people who would say

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.220
that building a super lifelike,

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.070
extremely intelligent
robot is a terrible idea,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.530
the kind of thing that could lead

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.273
to the downfall of the human species.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.820
But today, I will visit a
startup called Sanctuary A.I.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.461
They've built this crazy robot

00:02:33.461 --> 00:02:37.330
that has artificial
intelligence baked into it.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.080
And it also has these
very life-like hands.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.000
Sanctuary claims to have the smartest,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.070
most capable android on the planet.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.040
The only thing we can say for certain

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.090
is that because the robot
is being built in Canada,

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.373
it will be ridiculously polite.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.100
I'm sorry.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:58.773
Excuse me.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:00.573
My bad.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.250
Sanctuary A.I. was started

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.760
by a handful of engineers in 2019.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.893
The company's CEO is this fella,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.233
and its Chief Technology
Officer is Suzanne Gildert.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.790
If Suzanne looks familiar to some of you,

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.303
that's because she's been
on "Hello World" before.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.020
Her previous company was called Kindred,

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:31.420
and it made robotic arms

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.823
that could pick and
place items in factories.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.360
Suzanne's new company is a
lot like her old company,

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.253
only better.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:43.940
Do you wanna shake hands?

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:45.153
Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.033
Nice to meet you.

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:47.866
Nice to meet you.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:52.350
So this is a general-purpose robot.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.990
It's humanoid in form and function.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.960
It's designed to be good
at doing work tasks.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.276
Do I call it an it?

00:04:00.276 --> 00:04:01.890
How do we refer?

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.399
I usually refer to the robot as Eric.

00:04:04.399 --> 00:04:07.193
Eric is our pilot who you'll see

00:04:07.193 --> 00:04:09.400
taking control of the robot.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.690
He will be our operator today.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.014
Hi Eric.

00:04:13.014 --> 00:04:13.873
Hey.

00:04:20.937 --> 00:04:24.290
I think people have seen
robots at a car factory.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.290
But like, walk us through
some of the advances

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:30.393
you guys have made, you know,
versus that type of machine.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:36.080
Most robots you'll find in
industry are special purpose

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.590
and they're programmed
in a very different way.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.780
They're programmed to do a repetitive task

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.084
over and over again, so that robot,

00:04:44.084 --> 00:04:46.640
it doesn't really know where things are,

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:50.350
it's just executing some
pre-written routines.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.600
So what we're trying to
do here is build a robot

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.620
that is actually aware
of its surroundings,

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.370
that's building up a mental model

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.670
of everything it's seeing in real time,

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:01.350
and is therefore able
to do different tasks,

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.450
more general-purpose tasks.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:04.540
And then a big part of that

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.240
would be the dexterity of the hands.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.510
I know that you guys have
been focusing on that

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:10.370
for quite some time.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:11.800
Yeah, these are sort of about

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.600
the fourth-generation
hands that we've built.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.350
So maybe Eric, you can hold out your hand,

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.173
I can show some of the features.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:25.142
So obviously a human-like
hand, four fingers and a thumb.

00:05:25.142 --> 00:05:28.060
These things you see here are sensors.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.510
That gives the robot a
haptic sense of touch.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.720
When these pressure sensors are pressed,

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.510
the data signals go
into the software system

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:39.550
and they're also relayed to the pilot.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:40.480
So Eric has like,

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:41.330
a glove on.
Yes.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:42.369
And he can feel?
Yes.

00:05:42.369 --> 00:05:45.030
Eric, you can feel
that, when you're poked?

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:46.100
Uh, yeah.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:47.638
Why don't we play a game of chess.

00:05:47.638 --> 00:05:48.471
Okay.
And we can talk

00:05:48.471 --> 00:05:50.570
through some of the
components while we do that.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.020
All right, so you're white.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.598
You go first, right?

00:05:56.598 --> 00:05:58.480
Yeah, sounds good.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:05:59.951
Should have pre-planned my move.

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:04.040
I will be using the Sicilian defense,

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:05.481
just to let you know.

00:06:05.481 --> 00:06:07.517
Oh, seems I might be outmatched.

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:13.430
Eric, when you're doing this, I mean,

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:16.040
are you just seeing the piece like I am,

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.890
or there's some sort of guide,
like do you have a helmet on?

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:20.723
Yes, I do.

00:06:20.723 --> 00:06:21.960
I have a VR headset.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.317
So what I'm seeing is stereo vision

00:06:24.317 --> 00:06:26.680
through the camera you're looking at.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.900
Yeah, you can almost
see behind you, right?

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.540
Yeah.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.553
Oh, I didn't even see
the camera over here.

00:06:36.564 --> 00:06:38.887
I think that probably if people saw this

00:06:38.887 --> 00:06:40.430
for the first time and then they hear

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:42.780
that there's a human operator behind it,

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.090
they would kind of be like, "Oh, well,

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:47.285
I wanted the full robot."

00:06:47.285 --> 00:06:48.290
Yeah.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:50.590
So a lot of people ask, well, you know,

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:53.443
isn't this just a person
behind the scenes?

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:57.760
But having a person behind the scenes

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:01.890
allows us to gather lots of
very human-like training data

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.790
to train the software system,
train the A.I. algorithms

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:07.030
to be more human-like over time.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:09.620
So we're currently just using VR rigs.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.550
Off the shelf gear, but we're planning

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:13.050
on having some new rigs made

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:16.360
that actually give force
feedback to the pilot as well.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.190
So Eric, can you hold your arm out?

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:21.440
And if I did this, the
pilot would actually

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.470
be able to feel the
force feedback as well.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.530
These robots are designed
to work alongside people,

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.690
so we need them to be very safe.

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:32.252
And one of the things about
this robot and its motor system

00:07:32.252 --> 00:07:35.360
is it actually operates in
a force-controlled mode.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.410
So what that means is the robot

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.612
will try and hold the position of the arm,

00:07:39.612 --> 00:07:41.750
but if a maximum force is applied

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.857
that is greater than a
certain force, it will give.

00:07:45.857 --> 00:07:48.660
Tell me a little bit
about your background,

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:52.210
just 'cause I know you've
been into electronics

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:54.400
and computing since you were a kid, right?

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.260
Well, I've loved electronics and robotics

00:07:57.260 --> 00:07:59.513
and things like that since I was little.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.650
I did a degree in physics

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:06.970
and a Ph.D. in physics
and electronics, too.

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:10.680
And then from there, I worked
in the quantum computing space

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.763
for a while and eventually
found my way into A.I.

00:08:13.763 --> 00:08:15.480
You grew up in the UK?

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:16.313
Yeah, that's right.

00:08:16.313 --> 00:08:17.690
And I know like outside of this,

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:21.160
you have all these interesting
tech related pursuits.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:23.000
Yeah, so I have quite a lot

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.610
of interesting hobbies that I do.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.860
I do like to do paintings.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:30.610
They've kind of moved towards
a more tech electronics theme

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:33.690
and they're actually inspired
a lot by computer history.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:35.260
So a lot of the paintings you'll see

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:38.230
are things like close ups
of old microprocessors.

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:41.850
There are even a few that are
like robot power circuits.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:44.233
So always thinking about robots.

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:46.113
Whose move is it Eric?

00:08:46.113 --> 00:08:47.630
I believe it's your turn.

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:51.513
I'm not gonna lose to an A.I. human.

00:08:52.462 --> 00:08:53.295
Team human.

00:08:53.295 --> 00:08:54.540
Okay, okay.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:55.873
I'm okay with that strategy.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.760
Chess is a really interesting
game for several reasons.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:05.270
One is that it's fairly easy
now for software systems

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:08.231
to solve the intellectual part of chess.

00:09:08.231 --> 00:09:11.250
It's been historically used in A.I. many,

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.680
many, many times for teaching machines.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.970
What we're really trying
to show here, though,

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:20.580
is that it's the parts of
chess that people find easy,

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:22.840
like picking up a piece, moving it,

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:25.100
knowing where all the
pieces are on the board,

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:28.330
those are the parts that
people find very easy.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.900
Computer algorithms find
that part extremely hard.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.690
But also chess is a stand-in

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:35.390
for all different kinds of activities

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:37.610
that you might have to
do in the world of work.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:40.794
Maybe things need organizing
in the right locations.

00:09:40.794 --> 00:09:44.380
You need to take stock of
items in a retail store.

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:47.160
Or you could imagine
instead of chess pieces,

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:50.880
these were small parts on an
assembly or a sortation line.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.250
I mean, the goal is eventually then,

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:55.670
the robot is just smart enough,

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:56.840
it's just doing its own thing

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:58.834
with maybe no human operator in some jobs.

00:09:58.834 --> 00:10:00.560
Yeah, for certain tasks.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:04.370
So I'd like to see this robot
system be able to automate

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.830
the really mundane,
boring parts out of the--

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:10.920
Sorry, I've got to readjust something.

00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:17.307
I would appreciate if you
gave me a minute or two.

00:10:17.307 --> 00:10:18.810
I'll just let him adjust that

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.977
and move out of the wave his arm.

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:28.660
So I'd like to see these robots
doing the sort of boring,

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:31.770
mundane parts of tasks, and
then the pilots are now freed up

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.320
to be more like the overseers

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:35.710
or the managers of the group of robots.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:38.360
So you can imagine a group of
robots all being controlled

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:40.110
by a single pilot,

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:43.476
almost like the person
becomes more of a manager

00:10:43.476 --> 00:10:47.986
and the robots become more
like the manual workers.

00:10:47.986 --> 00:10:48.819
Okay.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:11:00.010
So about 95% of all jobs,
the dexterity of the fingers

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:03.260
precludes conventional robotic
systems from doing them.

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:08.260
So we have attempted to build
the world's best robotic hands

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:11.560
because hands are really
important for work.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:16.410
So we are working on integrating
state of the art robots,

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:19.590
specifically for manipulation
of the world through hands

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:23.150
with a new kind of A.I.
system that integrates

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:25.470
a lot of the tools that
people have been developing

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:27.780
around the world into a
platform that's intended

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:31.107
to give these robots the kind
of understanding of the world

00:11:31.107 --> 00:11:34.030
that is kind of a baseline for work.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:35.530
It's interesting because I think

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:36.750
there's definitely people who would look

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:39.290
at what you guys are making and
go, "Great, now you're going

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.720
to take all these factory
jobs from people."

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:44.080
Well, it's not, it's not taking jobs.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.810
In fact, I think that it's
starting to be recognized

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.500
that the problem that we face

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:50.410
in terms of the employment labor market

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.030
is actually the opposite problem.

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:55.040
There aren't enough people
to do the kinds of work

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:57.320
that needs to get done to support

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:00.343
the continued flourishing of
civilization, our way of life.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.910
There are more than 11
million unfilled positions

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:07.310
in the US right now.

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:10.150
The world needs more workers,

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:11.900
especially in the kinds of jobs

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:13.720
that people typically don't want to do,

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:16.270
those 11 million open jobs right now.

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:18.450
Those are the kinds of things
that we're going to target,

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:19.660
and that's a big number.

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:23.080
And 11 million jobs, it's
only going to grow over time.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:26.320
Birth rates are plummeting
and 11 million robots

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.750
of our sort in the world doing those jobs

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:31.040
doesn't take a single
job away from anyone,

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.700
but 11 million robots out in the world

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:35.080
is an enormous business.

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:36.480
It's starting to become

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:38.810
at the scale of the automotive industry.

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:43.810
And I do believe that this is
the birth of a new industry.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:47.400
It sort of looks like robotics

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.200
and it sort of looks like A.I.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.270
But it's a new thing
and it's going to rival

00:12:52.270 --> 00:12:54.200
the biggest industries there are today

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.593
in importance and scale
when it gets going.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:04.090
Okay, so you're moving in on me quick.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:06.548
Distracting me with your fancy hands.

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:09.780
I'm going to deal with your knight

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:11.120
because it's really bothering me.

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:13.390
Ah, my knight.

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:15.760
Yeah, he's a troublemaker.

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:16.770
Yeah.

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:18.910
What did you get up to this weekend?

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:21.900
You know, just robot
stuff, a lot of chess.

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:24.510
I was practicing for you.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:25.810
I know you're pretty good.

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:31.610
Ooh, I don't like that.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:35.483
I will take this and put it over here.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.603
Not cool, not cool.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:42.250
Oh yeah.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:43.653
You don't miss a beat man.

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:48.000
All right, I will concede.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.066
I can see you've got me beat.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:54.290
Very good game, well played.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:55.683
Thank you sir, thank you.

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:00.741
So we saw Eric as the pilot

00:14:00.741 --> 00:14:03.610
picking up the chess pieces quite easily,

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:05.733
using his human mind.

00:14:05.733 --> 00:14:09.700
And here the robot is trying
to learn what it means

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:11.700
to actually pick up a chess piece

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:13.440
in the same way that a human would.

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:16.393
And it does it by trying
over and over and over again.

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:23.200
So on this screen over here,
you can see a close up of it

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:24.960
trying to grab a chess
piece and you'll see

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:27.910
it sort of fumbles around
a little bit at the start.

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:29.766
But after enough training episodes,

00:14:29.766 --> 00:14:32.970
it will learn to grab it
in a very human-like way.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:36.977
There's not obviously 10,000 robot arms.

00:14:36.977 --> 00:14:38.570
This is all simulated.

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:41.170
Yeah, so here, we can have thousands

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:43.240
or hundreds of thousands of hands

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:45.950
trying to pick things up
faster than real time.

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.898
So you're accelerating the learning rate

00:14:48.898 --> 00:14:51.360
by millions of times.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:54.160
So the physics engine, the
rules that have been put

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:56.910
into the software to mimic like gravity

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:59.870
and the different properties of our world

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:04.720
are good enough that this is
actually training the hand.

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:06.740
Yeah, they're getting there.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:10.520
So if you think about where
you could have done this many,

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:11.910
like a few years ago,
you might think, well,

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:13.960
"I could use a game engine maybe"

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:16.440
because game engines are
pretty good simulated worlds.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:18.710
But then when you get
deep into game engines,

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:21.350
you find they're sort of
optimized to make games look good.

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:23.550
They have a lot of animations in them.

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:25.750
They don't have a lot of real physics.

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:29.120
So we're actually bootstrapping
on game engine technology,

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.150
but there are now
systems going beyond that

00:15:31.150 --> 00:15:33.320
and trying to mimic physics very well.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:35.410
And so even something like the interaction

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:37.490
of the end of a fingertip with an object

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:39.070
has to be modeled very well.

00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:41.463
So you need a really good physics engine.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:45.870
What do we have here?

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:50.360
Yeah, so we saw the grasping
algorithm being trained.

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:51.690
What we're actually seeing here

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:53.353
are those grasps being realized.

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:59.180
It's taking what it's learned
and putting it into practice.

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:02.020
So it's motion planning,
not just the grasp anymore,

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:03.280
but that in combination

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.593
with the entire movement
of the robot body.

00:16:06.920 --> 00:16:10.670
Your eyes kinda lit up when
you were talking about this.

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:11.503
Why?

00:16:11.503 --> 00:16:14.610
Yeah, so when you just look
at this from the outside,

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:17.890
it's like, "Oh, a virtual
robot is moving a chess piece.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:18.723
So what?"

00:16:18.723 --> 00:16:21.840
But to me, this is like
a spark of life moment

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:23.350
because what you're actually seeing

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:26.180
is fully autonomous grasping

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:29.000
and manipulation of an object in a scene

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.200
where none of this is pre
programmed or specified.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.380
It's figuring out where those pieces are

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:36.620
just by looking at the
scene, looking at them.

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:37.760
You can see in this view,

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:41.880
it's actually trying
different potential grasps.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:43.700
And then it's actually executing the move

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:45.650
using real physics.

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:47.626
Okay, you've got this virtual robot

00:16:47.626 --> 00:16:49.890
getting more sophisticated
and doing these things.

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:50.880
But like, in my head,

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:53.710
I feel like the second
you have the real robot,

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:55.010
there's going to be all these differences

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:55.843
and the chess piece

00:16:55.843 --> 00:16:58.150
and the physics between
the hand and the pieces.

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.310
There are still some discrepancies

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:02.760
between reality and the simulation,

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:05.410
but that gap is closing
all the time and to me,

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:07.260
this could transfer and generalize

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:10.283
to other types of
objects, other scenarios.

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:13.710
So rather than just
thinking about a chess game,

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:16.310
think about cutlery being
laid out in a restaurant

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:17.460
or something like that.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:23.875
So what we're seeing now is we are seeing

00:17:23.875 --> 00:17:26.191
a little bit of a peek inside

00:17:26.191 --> 00:17:27.920
the A.I. mind when it's running.

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:28.753
Okay.

00:17:28.753 --> 00:17:29.940
So if you remember earlier,

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:33.030
we saw the VR type of piloting.

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:35.380
Imagine now, instead of
having to control the robot

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:36.380
with all this VR gear,

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:39.110
you just have a dropdown list of options.

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:42.550
So you can see there are two screens here.

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:45.140
This one we call the outer world model.

00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:47.840
So this is a digital twin of reality.

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:50.080
This is a simulation of the world

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:51.623
and it's a simulated robot.

00:17:51.623 --> 00:17:55.790
Now, this one down here is
the robot's mental model

00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:58.057
of the world, so this is inside its mind.

00:17:58.057 --> 00:17:58.910
Okay.

00:17:58.910 --> 00:18:00.750
And this is outside its mind.

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:01.970
So you'll see the main difference

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:03.820
between these two at the
moment, it's pretty obvious.

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:05.550
There is a chessboard with the pieces here

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:06.500
and it's not here.

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:08.220
So the reason is that the robot

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:09.837
is not looking at the chessboard.

00:18:09.837 --> 00:18:10.760
Okay.

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:12.368
And it's never seen it before,

00:18:12.368 --> 00:18:15.990
so it doesn't have a mental
model of the chessboard.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:18.720
But say I'm now a pilot here.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:22.140
I can use the command "look down" here.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:24.580
We're now controlling the
simulated robot look down.

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:27.650
And now you can see the
chessboard appeared here.

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:31.511
So now this, it's building
up this inner mental picture

00:18:31.511 --> 00:18:33.094
of what's going on in real time.

00:18:33.094 --> 00:18:35.080
Yeah, they're all like moving around.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:37.330
Yeah, so they're jostling
around a little bit.

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:40.071
And the reason is, is that
these are all being detected

00:18:40.071 --> 00:18:43.523
and figured out what
they are in real time.

00:18:43.523 --> 00:18:46.910
Okay so this is super
interesting what happened.

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:50.010
Down here, a bunch of the
pieces have disappeared.

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:51.550
It's like blocking it's vision, or...

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:54.590
Yeah, so see the hand is
in front of the pieces?

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:57.140
I think it's like a six month old baby.

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:59.470
If you show it something
and then you hide it--

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:02.130
Like peekaboo.
It thinks it's gone.

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:05.480
So, that's a very human-like
trait of a developing mind

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:08.100
is to go through this stage
where if you can't see it,

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:09.950
it doesn't exist.

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:12.826
So what we really want to
get to here is this robot

00:19:12.826 --> 00:19:15.471
in any real-world scenario
would roll into a room,

00:19:15.471 --> 00:19:17.620
it would look around and
it would start building

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:21.060
a mental model of everything
it was seeing in real time,

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:22.895
including the room and all the objects

00:19:22.895 --> 00:19:25.797
and what they are and
where they're located.

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:36.100
It's funny because in some ways,

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:38.670
I'm picturing my head like 1960s sci-fi,

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:41.330
like an android that is moving around.

00:19:41.330 --> 00:19:44.130
And what you guys are making
is still sort of in some ways

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:47.200
people's first conception of
where all this was heading.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:48.117
Well, I think so, yeah.

00:19:48.117 --> 00:19:50.400
And I like to think of Sanctuary's mission

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:53.460
as going back to the sort
of original vision of A.I.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:56.240
when people like Alan
Turing and Marvin Minsky

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:57.677
and these people were thinking about,

00:19:57.677 --> 00:20:00.540
"How do we actually
make a machine or system

00:20:00.540 --> 00:20:03.667
that thinks and behaves like a person?"

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:08.920
If we want systems to be able to work

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:10.130
in the same way people do,

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:12.640
interact with us in
the same way people do,

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:14.530
they need a human-like body

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:17.274
and a very human-like intelligence.

00:20:17.274 --> 00:20:20.980
And you think the
dystopian stuff is all...

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:25.973
You're optimistic by nature or
you think it's all overblown?

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.630
If we manage to develop
fully autonomous A.I.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:32.477
in a general-purpose robot, you might ask,

00:20:32.477 --> 00:20:34.193
"What will that look like?

00:20:35.374 --> 00:20:36.960
How will it change the world?

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:38.410
What's going to be going on?"

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:42.030
But you saw it earlier, right,

00:20:42.030 --> 00:20:45.279
it'll look exactly like it
did when Eric was piloting.

00:20:45.279 --> 00:20:49.390
It's just there won't be a
person in a VR suit in there.

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:51.050
So I would ask you, well,

00:20:51.050 --> 00:20:54.490
was that scary and
terrifying and dystopian?

00:20:54.490 --> 00:20:55.610
Right.

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:57.610
And I also think if you are focusing

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:01.320
on a very optimistic future
where you have robots

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.860
working alongside people, helping people,

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:06.720
I think you will make
that future come to pass.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:09.030
You get people accusing you
of being naively optimistic.

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:11.240
But I think you need
that if you want to make

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:14.940
that future real and if you're
really optimistic about it,

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:17.180
it's more likely to happen.

00:21:17.180 --> 00:21:19.450
I promise I'm smiling behind the camera.

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:21.520
Do robots say cheese?

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:22.790
Yeah, absolutely.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:25.207
Alright, one, two, three.

00:21:25.207 --> 00:21:26.319
Cheese.
Cheese!

